{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact clean pushed head 36d91a708be16d72e01da62396bf628273a4318b, against the complete six-artifact set 2135c5ea cmd/gs, ea0a557c internal/app, c57f8942 internal/residentclient, 4805bde5 internal/service, 841f0b1c docs/reference/architecture.md and baf9a085 docs/reference/gs/serve.md, under planner request 37412523.\n\nThe design is sound and most of it is well proved. Two things block, and both are about a wrong answer being unrecoverable rather than about the code being wrong today.\n\nBLOCKER ONE — the one branch whose failure cannot be undone is the one branch with no red proof. internal/app/resident.go:347, \"if answer.Liveness == Dead\", is the single line stopping a hung incumbent from being handed a vacancy proof. I mutated it to \"!= Alive\", built clean, and ran go test ./internal/app/ -count=1 myself: exit 0, ok, 104.3s. The guard has no failing test.\n\nWhy it matters more than an ordinary coverage gap: the post-bind CAS cannot save you here. A hung incumbent — accepting connections, never answering — probes Ambiguous. If that line loosens, a vacancy proof is issued carrying observed=X, the starter binds a different port, and the CAS X-\u003eY succeeds precisely because a hung process never moves its ref. Two residents on one log, which is the split room this whole mechanism exists to prevent. Every other guard here fails safe; this one fails silently and permanently.\n\nPlease add the test the comment already implies: preflight against a claim whose prober returns Ambiguous must return *ResidentHeldError and leave the ref untouched.\n\nBLOCKER TWO — the version-skew path advises deleting a live claim. /v0/identity now carries \"pid\" (internal/service/server.go:154-157), and the probe decodes with DisallowUnknownFields (internal/residentclient/client.go:179). I read both sides at both revisions rather than inferring: at current main the client's identity struct is {genesis} alone; at this head it is {genesis, pid}. So a pre-change gs serve probing a post-change resident gets an unknown-field decode error, which is not ECONNREFUSED, so Ambiguous, so a refusal whose recovery text ends \"only then remove the claim with git update-ref -d refs/gitseq/resident/\u003cgenesis\u003e\". An operator who follows that advice during a rollout deletes a live claim and starts the second resident.\n\n/v0/identity is the one endpoint whose entire job is agreement between two independently built binaries, and nothing tests either direction across versions. The new \"first prove no service is answering\" wording genuinely mitigates this and I credit it, but it is advice against a mistake the tooling invites. Decoding this one probe leniently, with a fixture asserting {\"genesis\":...,\"pid\":...,\"future\":...} still probes Alive, would remove the invitation.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are cmd/gs, internal/app, internal/residentclient and internal/service. This IS a contract change: app.Prober's signature moves from Liveness to ResidentProbe, and CheckResident, ClaimResidentAfter, ResidentVacancy, ResidentProbe and ResidentOwnership.Reclaimed are new exported surface. docs/reference/architecture.md and docs/reference/gs/serve.md are updated in the same head and carry candidate artifacts 841f0b1c and baf9a085, so the contract-change requirement in AGENTS.md step 4 is met.\n\nSECURITY. I examined the takeover authority boundary directly and it holds. The vacancy proof is genuinely one-use: residentVacancyProof is unexported, only CheckResident constructs one, ResidentVacancy carries a pointer so a struct copy shares the same atomic.Bool, and consumed.CompareAndSwap(false,true) at :397 spends the bit BEFORE the CAS attempt, so a missed CAS still burns it. Repository binding at :394 checks commonDir and ref. Takeover CASes on the exact observed OID, closing the read-to-act window. Ambiguous never authorizes takeover; unreadable, malformed and foreign claims refuse rather than count as vacancy. I traced the crash-and-restart race — incumbent dies, a starter observes vacancy, the owner's supervisor restarts and re-claims first — and the exact-OID CAS resolves it correctly, with the loser falling back to a fresh probe that now answers Alive.\n\nThe probe boundary also holds. ValidateURL rejects non-http, credentials, query, fragment and non-root paths, and requires localhost or a loopback IP; a 28-URL battery including ::ffff:169.254.169.254, 2130706433, 0177.0.0.1, 127.1, 127.0.0.1.nip.io and credential- and fragment-smuggling shapes found nothing that reaches a non-loopback host. Redirects are refused, and Go never proxies loopback, so HTTP_PROXY cannot redirect the probe either. Only ECONNREFUSED yields Dead; I enumerated every other exit from ProbeResident — validation failure, non-refused transport error, HTTPError, ReadError, size limit, decode failure, trailing JSON, empty or mismatched genesis — and all return Ambiguous. No branch turns a live resident into Dead.\n\nThree security notes that do not block. The refusal PID is the answering service's self-report; the code correctly never authorizes on it and offers no kill advice, but serve.md should say it is the service's word rather than an observed fact. heldResident %s-formats incumbent.URL and readResidentClaim only checks it non-empty, so a claim blob carrying ANSI escapes prints raw to a terminal beside deletion advice — %q would close it, and it needs repo write access to reach, so it is low. And TestProbeResidentRefusesToDialAnythingButLoopback asserts only that the verdict is Ambiguous, which a failed non-loopback dial also produces, so it is vacuous as a no-dial proof; a recording dialer would make it real.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The one-primitive framing — acquisition, takeover and release are all a ref CAS carrying the expected old value — is the right shape and I would not change it. Two smaller things. errors.Join at resident.go:533 is presented as joining cleanup failures, but \"last\" is overwritten each iteration, so only the final error survives; either accumulate or drop the claim from the comment. And Release has no attempt bound, exiting only on success or ctx.Done, while every test passes context.Background: the successor-preservation guard does go red under mutation, but as a package-timeout panic rather than an assertion, which reads like infrastructure trouble. Bounding attempts would turn that hang into a failure message.\n\nVERIFICATION. Baseline at the pristine head, go test ./cmd/gs/ ./internal/app/ ./internal/residentclient/ ./internal/service/ -count=1, exit 0. I personally re-ran ./internal/app/ under the F1 mutation and watched it stay green; the wider cmd/gs measurement came from the deep pass rather than from my own hands, and I am marking which is which rather than presenting them as one. Tree restored and git status --porcelain empty at 36d91a70 afterwards.\n\nNineteen guards do have named failing tests under mutation, including the one-use proof, the exact-OID CAS, the CAS-miss fallback, preflight read-only, the Alive refusal withholding deletion advice, ECONNREFUSED-only Dead, the no-redirect policy, strict JSON, release-on-signal ordering and the real-binary SIGINT and SIGTERM subtests. That is a strong set and it is why only one missing proof stands out.\n\nUnrelated housekeeping for whoever merges: the main checkout carries pre-existing untracked package.json and package-lock.json, which gs merge refuses; move them aside and restore them after.\n\nDo not merge.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2135c5ea851f9685a0883bf69c03d3e9cfe4c4fe","head":"36d91a708be16d72e01da62396bf628273a4318b","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7b6e32f2328c9e2dc4b255fb9d189c57bc0d0f86\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7b6e32f2328c9e2dc4b255fb9d189c57bc0d0f86","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}